    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Kayhan Kalhor & Kiya Tabassian"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\n                “Never was there a man who sought so much” \n700 years since the death of Marco Polo\nMarco Polo did not go to Asia for wealth, power, and honours, but to discover a form of civilization. The Travels of Marco Polo narrates a journey of knowledge more than a conquest. “Never was there a man who sought so much” draws inspiration from this belief, creating a concert-reading of music, images, and words that follows the diverse routes of Marco Polo’s travels in the Far East, including a passage through Persian lands. Alternating with the pages in Franco-Venetian language from The Travels, selected and read by Sandro Cappelletto, are the musical improvisations of two masters of Persian music: Kayhan Kalhor, the legendary kamancheh player, a bowed instrument of the Iranian tradition, and Kiya Tabassian, a setar player, the Persian lute.\n            "
      }
    ],
